rnment put the bill on hold DWS  matías boyega is in Hong Kong mattias  thanks for joining us what exactly are  people trying to achieve today well  today at the score of this is still this  extradition bill that is shelved but not  withdrawn by the government but the  whole issue has become much broader it's  about the governance of Hong Kong people  want to make sure that their voice  continues to be heard they want to be  they want to be on the agenda they do  not want the government get away with  just postponing the bill and not making  any other changes so today is really  more of a statement that says we are  still here they are marching in Kowloon  on the Kowloon side of Hong Kong this is  an area that is popular with Chinese  tourists they are marching towards a  train station where the trains from  China arrive so it is on the one hand  also  a way of raising or an F attempt to  raise awareness among mainland Chinese  who have been caught off by their own  media from these protests right you know  as you well know on Monday similar  protests turned violent when protesters  ransacked the legislature what can we  expect today well people do not expect  violence today you could see this right  away at the beginning people were coming  bringing their children elderly people  were joining this is not the environment  where violence happens the organizers of  this March individuals politicians who  have got the the police approval for  this March they have also said there  will be no violence today it's about  making a statement it's awesome about  making a statement that Monday's  storming of the legislature was a  punctual action an action that targeted  only the legislature and that this vile  this movement is not turning into a  broader rioting movement or anything  Matthias Bollinger in Hong Kong thank  you so much
 

